/*! \page developer_backend_id_page Backend Identifiers

\ref developer_backend_page       (Prev/Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_field_page (Next)    &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

PSCF uses C++ templates to define closely analogous classes that use
different computational backends.

\section developer_backend_id_template_sec Template Patterns

All PSCF classes and functions that may be specialized to allow the use
of more than one possible computational backend are defined as templates
that accept a special kind of typename template parameter that is used
to identify a choice of computational backend.
A class that is designed to be used only in this role, as an argument
of a template parameter that identifies a specific choice of backend,
is described as a backend identifier classes. PSCF defines one such
class for each available backend, i.e., one to identify specializations
that use a CPU backend and one that to identify specializations that use
a GPU and contain CUDA code. A template parameter that is designed to
take such a backend identifier classes as its argument is referred to
as a backend identifier parameter. By convention, the symbol "T" is
always used for a backend identifier parameter in any template
definitions.

Many of the class templates that accept a backend identifier template
parameter also take the integer dimension of spaced as an additional
parameter parameter. The dimension of space is always denoted by an
int template parameter named D. In such templates, parameter D is
always listed as the first template parameter, and a backend template
parameter T as a second.  Almost all of the class templates defined in
namespace Pscf::Rp are templates with these two template parameters.

<b> Example </b>: The Pscf::Rp::System class template, which represents
the highest level object in a SCFT or PS-SCFT calculation, is declared
within the Pscf::Rp namespace by a template declared as
\code
namespace Pscf {
   namespace Rp {
      template <int D, class T> class System;
   }
}
\endcode
An instantiation of this template must use an integer argument D=1, 2,
or 3 for the int parameter D, and one of the template identifier classes
as the argument for the class parameter T.

\section developer_backend_id_class_sec Backend Identifier Classes

PSCF currently defines two backend identifier classes, named Pscf:CPT
and PSCF::CUT. Use of one of these classes as the argument of a backend
identifier template parameter identifies a particular choice of
computational backend. Within code defined in the Pscf namespace:

  - Class CPT identifies a C++ CPU backend

  - Class CUT identifies a CUDA GPU backend

Instances of template specializations that take Pscf::CPT as a template
parameter argument are used to construct the pscf_rpc CPU program, whose
that that take Pscf::CUT as an argument are used to construct the pscf_rpg
program.

<b> Example </b>: The class template specialization
\code
namespace Pscf {
   Rp::System <3, CUT>
}
\endcode
is a specialization of the Pscf::Rp::System class template that is
designed to use a CUDA GPU backend to treat three-dimensional systems.

When the pscf_rpg program is invoked with a command line option "-d 3",
indicating D=3, the main program constructs an instance of the class
Pscf::Rp::System\<3, CUT\> and then invokes member functions of that
object to perform all subsequent operations.

\section developer_backend_id_enum_sec BackendId Enum Class

PSCF also defines an enum class known as Pscf::BackendId for which
different enum values represent different choices of backend.
The Pscf::BackendId enum class is defined as follows:
\code
namespace Pscf {

   enum class BackendId {
      Cpp, Cuda, None
   };

}
\endcode
The value BackendId::Cpp indicates a C++ / Cpu backend, while the
value BackendId::Cuda indicates a Cuda / Gpu backend.

The BackendId enum class and a backend identifier class are designed
to be used to identify a choice of backend in different contexts: The
value of a variable of type BackendId can be tested to identify the
backend at run time.  A backend identifier class is instead designed
to be used only at compile time, as a template argument that identifies
a particular backend during compilation.

The backend identifier classes CPT and CUT each define a const static
member named "id" that is a variable of type BackendId. The value of
CPT::id is BackendId::Cpp, while the value of CUT::id is BackendId::Cuda.
In template code that uses a template parameter T to denote a backend
identifier class, the value of T::id can be tested to explicitly
identify the backend at run time.

<BR>
